[FINAL-TEXT] I now have the complete picture. The log tells the whole story: ospfd on R6 started, formed adjacencies with R4 and R5, then at 21:36:07 it **crashed** ("encountered an error and is shutting down"), removing 64 OSPF routes from the RIB. And critically, **watchfrr is not running** on R6 — so ospfd was never restarted.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r6
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on **R6**, not R7. R6's `ospfd` process crashed at 21:36:07 (lab time) and was never restarted because `watchfrr` is also absent from R6's process list. The FRR log on R6 explicitly records: *"Client 'ospf' (session id 0) encountered an error and is shutting down"*, followed immediately by Zebra withdrawing all 64 of R6's OSPF routes from the kernel RIB.

The cascading effect explains everything the ticket describes: R7 sits exclusively on segment Y (the R6–R7 link, 67.0.0.0/8), sharing it only with R6's `eth2`. With R6's ospfd dead, R6 can no longer send OSPF Hellos on that segment, so R7's only potential OSPF neighbor is permanently silent. R7's own `ospfd` is fully operational — its interfaces are up, it is sending Hellos and has elected itself DR — but it cannot form any adjacency and thus cannot inject its loopbacks (17.1.x.1/24), the R6–R7 link subnet (67.0.0.0/8), or its LAN (70.0.0.0/8) into the OSPF domain. Those routes consequently vanish from every router in the topology, including R1.

R6's own unique prefixes (16.x.x.x loopbacks, 60.0.0.0/8 on segment Z) are also missing from the domain for the same reason; the team only noticed the R7 side because R6's shared links (46.0.0.0/8, 56.0.0.0/8) remain advertised by R4 and R5 respectively.

**Root cause:** R6's `ospfd` was killed (watchfrr absent, so no auto-restart), collapsing R6's OSPF participation and starving R7 of any adjacency.

**Fix:** Restart ospfd on R6 (`service frr restart` or `watchfrr`-supervised restart). Ensure watchfrr is running on R6 going forward so ospfd is automatically recovered on future failures.
=== END ===